# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3416> (FET protein family)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3416> "Members of the FET protein family, consisting of FUS, EWSR1, and TAF15, bind to RNA and contribute to the control of transcription, RNA processing, and the cytoplasmic fates of messenger RNAs in metazoa. FET proteins can also bind DNA, which may be important in transcription and DNA damage responses. FET proteins are of medical interest because chromosomal rearrangements of their genes promote various sarcomas and because point mutations in FUS or TAF15 can cause neurodegenerative diseases such as amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and frontotemporal lobar dementia.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3416> "FUS/EWS/TAF15 Protein Family")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3416> "FET protein family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3416> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3429> (Hexokinase family)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3429> "Hexokinases phosphorylate a variety of hexose sugars, although their preferred substrate is glucose. GCK has a single hexokinase domain while the other family members have two hexokinase domains but only HK2 has been shown to have two active sites.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3429> "HK")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3429> "Hexokinase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3429> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3439> (RIG-I RNA sensor family)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3439> "The RIGI family of RNA sensors are pattern recognition receptors that recognize pathogen RNA and trigger an innate immune response.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3439> "RIG-I-like receptors")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3439> "RLRs")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3439> "retinoic acid-inducible gene-I (RIG-I)-like receptors")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3439> "RIG-I RNA sensor family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3439> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> (NALCN channel complex subunits)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> "NALCN regulates the resting membrane potential by mediating the Na+ leak current in neurons, and it functions as a channelosome in complex with NALF1, UNC79, and UNC80. Dysfunction of the NALCN channelosome causes a broad range of neurological and developmental diseases called NALCN channelopathies in humans.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> "NALCN channelosome")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> "NALCN-FAM155A-UNC79-UNC80 channel complex")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> "NALCN channel complex subunits")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> (Ribonucleotide reductase subunits)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> "The importance of ribonucleotide reductase (RNR) lies in its unique ability to catalyze the rate-limiting step of the reduction of ribonucleotides (NDPs) to the corresponding deoxyribonucleotides (dNDPs), the precursors of DNA. Mammalian RNRs consist of two homodimeric subunits, which associate to form the holoenzyme. The large RRM1 dimer contains the catalytic site, whereas the smaller dimer (RRM2 or RRM2B) contains an oxygen-requiring di-iron tyrosyl-radical site, which is essential for catalysis.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> "RNRs")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> "RRM")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> "Ribonucleoside-diphosphate reductases")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> "Ribonucleotide reductase subunits")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> (DMPK kinase family)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> "The DMPK kinases are members of the AGC kinase family with a conserved domain architecture of N-terminal kinase domains followed by a predicted coiled-coil domain and C-terminal regulatory domains that mediate a variety of protein-protein and protein-membrane interactions. While many AGC kinases are regulated by phosphorylation of motifs in their activation loop or C-terminal tail, the DMPK kinases are not. Instead, their N-terminal capped-helix bundle (CHB) domain cooperates with the C-terminal tail to maintain the kinase domain in a homodimeric, active conformation with an ordered activation loop. Protein kinases of the DMPK family are critical regulators of actomyosin contractility in cells.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> "DMPK family")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> "dystonia myotonica protein kinase family")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> "DMPK kinase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3496> (L-lactate dehydrogenase family)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3496> "L-lactate dehydrogenase (LDH, EC1.1.1.27) is a key enzyme that catalyzes the interconversion of L-lactate and pyruvate with nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD+) as a coenzyme. This enzyme exists in a tetrameric form by combination of M (muscle, LDHA) and H (heart, LDHB) subunits in various proportions in different somatic tissues. There are five major tetrameric LDH complexes consisting of LDHA and/or LDHB subunits. Expression of LDHC, LDHAL6A and the intronless gene LDHAL6B is restricted to testes where they may play a role in spermatogenesis.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3496> "L-lactate dehydrogenase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3496> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3503> (2-arachidonoylglycerol hydrolases)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3503> "The endocannabinoid 2-arachidonoylglycerol (2-AG) is a lipid mediator involved in various physiological processes. In response to neural activity, 2-AG is synthesized post-synaptically, then activates pre-synaptic type 1 cannabinoid recep  tors, resulting in transient and long-lasting reduction of neurotransmitter release. The signalling competence of 2-AG is tightly regulated by the balanced action between 'on demand' biosynthesis and degradation. The major enzymatic route for 2-AG inactivation in brain is via hydrolysis; three serine hydrolases (MGLL, BHD6 AND ABHD12) account for approx. 99% of brain 2-AG hydrolase activity.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3503> "2AG hydrolases")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3503> "2-arachidonoylglycerol hydrolases")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3503> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> (MCU complex subunits)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> "The mitochondrial calcium uniporter channel exists as a channel composed of a total of 4 MCU subunits and up to 4 SMDT1 subunits. In some cases, the dominant-negative MCU paralog, MCUB, replaces MCU subunits within the complex. The channel is gated by dimers of MICU1/2 or, in some tissues, dimers of MICU1/3. The accessory subunit MCUR1 also binds to and regulates the channel. Two uniporter channels can dimerize, and the function of these dimers is regulated by interactions between the MICU2 subunit associated with each channel.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> "MCUC")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> "mitochondrial calcium uniporter channel")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> "mitochondrial calcium uniporter complex")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> "MCU complex subunits")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3513> (Putative lysine methyltransferases)

AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3513> "Putative lysine methyltransferases")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3513> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3350>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3517> (Transaminases)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3517> "Transaminases (or Aminotransferases) (Enzyme Commission [EC] 2.6.1.-) are pyridoxal 5′-phosphate–dependent enzymes that catalyze the transamination reactions between amino acid donor and keto acid acceptor substrates.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3517> "Aminotransferases")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3517> "Transaminases")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3517> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3519> (Glutamine--fructose-6-phosphate transaminase family)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3519> "The key enzyme in the de novo production of UDP-GlcNAc through the hexosamine biosynthesis pathway is glutamine--fructose-6-phosphate amidotransferase (GFPT). GFPT is present in all cellular organisms, including prokaryotes and eukaryotes, underscoring the importance of this enzyme and the HBP for normal cell function. The two mammalian paralogs, GFPT1 and GFPT2, share 75–80% amino acid sequence identity. GFPT1 is found more ubiquitously, whereas GFPT2 seems to have higher expression in tissues of the central nervous system. GFPT comprises an N-terminal 27 kDa glutaminase domain that catalyzes the hydrolysis of glutamine to glutamate and ammonia. The C-terminus harbors the 40 kDa isomerase domains that use ammonia to convert fructose-6-P (a glycolytic intermediate) to glucosamine-6-phosphate (GlcN-6-P). This reaction is the rate-limiting step of the de novo synthesis of hexosamines.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3519> "GFPT")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3519> "Glutamine--fructose-6-phosphate transaminase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3519> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3517>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3520> (Glutamic--pyruvic transaminase family)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3520> "Glutamic--pyruvic transaminase (also known as alanine aminotransferase) catalyzes the reversible transamination between alanine and 2-oxoglutarate to generate pyruvate and glutamate, playing a key role in the intermediary metabolism of glucose and amino acids. There are two isoforms in human encoded by GPT and GPT2 that show differential expression and subcellular localisation.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3520> "ALTs")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3520> "Alanine aminotransferases")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3520> "Alanine transaminases")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3520> "GPT")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3520> "Glutamic--pyruvic transaminase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3520> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3517>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3533> (Choline kinase family)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3533> "Choline kinases catalyse the conversion of choline to phosphocholine, which is further converted to cytidine diphosphate-coline (CDP-choline) in the biosynthesis of phosphatidylcholine (PC). PC is a major constituent of the phospholipid bilayer which constitutes the eukaryotic cell membrane, and regulates cell signal transduction. Functional choline kinase exists as a dimer, and can form homodimers or heterodimers with differential dynamics dependent on the tissue type. CHKA homodimers display higher enzymatic activity than CHKB homodimers and CHKA/B heterodimers with tissues differing in their relative expression of the varying isoforms.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3533> "CHK")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3533> "Choline kinase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3533> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3556> (Phosphofructokinase family)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3556> "Phosphofructokinase (PFK) catalyses the phosphorylation of fructose 6-phosphate (F6P) to fructose 1,6-bisphosphate (F1,6BP) and plays a central role in the glycolytic pathway. The human enzymes are encoded by 3 paralogous genes: PFKM, PFKL and PFKP. While the isoforms were named for the tissue that they are prevalent or originally found (muscle, liver and platelet respectively) they are not limited to these tissues. The functional enzyme is a tetramer and can be formed from one or more isoforms. The kinetic/regulatory properties vary with subunit composition.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3556> "6-phosphofructo-1-kinase subunits")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3556> "PFK")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3556> "Phosphofructokinase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3556> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

