# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567> (Dynamin superfamily)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567> "The dynamin superfamily comprises an assortment of multi-domain GTPases, found from bacteria to man, that are distinguished from typical GTPases of the Ras, Rab and G-protein families by their modular structure, relatively large size (>70 kDa), and low affinity for guanine nucleotides. In addition, they display a conserved propensity to self-assemble into polymeric arrays, the dynamics of which are regulated by an autonomous, assembly-stimulated GTPase activity.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567> "Dynamin and dynamin-like GTPases")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567> "Dynamin superfamily")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3565>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> (Dynamins)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> "Based on domain architecture, the dynamin superfamily is broadly divided into two groups, classical dynamins and dynamin-related proteins. The common features shared by all dynamins and Drps are the presence of a large N-terminal GTPase domain, a middle domain (MD) and a GTPase effector domain (GED). While GTPase domain binds and hydrolyses GTP, MD and GED play important roles in oligomerization and regulation of GTPase activity. Classical dynamins contains two additional domains namely, pleckstrin homology domain (PHD) involved in membrane binding and a proline-rich domain (PRD) containing several SH3-binding sites important for protein–protein interactions.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> "Classical dynamins")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> "DNM")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> "Dynamin family")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> "Dynamins")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> (Translational GTPases)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> "Translational GTPases (trGTPases) play key roles in facilitating protein synthesis on the ribosome. Despite the high degree of evolutionary conservation in the sequences of their GTP-binding domains, the rates of GTP hydrolysis and nucleotide exchange vary broadly between different trGTPases.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> "protein-synthesizing GTPases")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> "trGTPases")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> "Translational GTPases")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3565>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> (Pyrroline-5-carboxylate reductase family)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> "PYCR1, PYCR2, and PYCR3 are members of the pyrroline-5-carboxylate reductase family of enzymes. They catalyze the final step of proline synthesis by converting pyrroline-5-carboxylate (P5C) into proline (EC 1.5.1.2). PYCR1 and PYCR2 localize to mitochondria, where their P5C substrate is generated from glutamate by P5C Synthase (P5CS), whereas PYCR3 is found in the cytosol and its P5C substrate is generated from ornithine by ornithine aminotransferase (OAT). PYCR1 and PYCR2 are both associated with human diseases, but with very different clinical manifestations. PYCR1 mutations cause Cutis Laxa (CL, MIM #612940 and 614438). In contrast, PYCR2 mutations cause hypomyelinating leukodystrophy type 10 (HLD10, MIM #616420). There are no diseases yet associated with PYCR3.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> "PYCR")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> "Pyrroline-5-carboxylate reductases")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> "Pyrroline-5-carboxylate reductase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3602> (Serine palmitoyltransferase complex subunits)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3602> "The first step of de novo sphingolipid biosynthesis is catalyzed by the serine palmitoyltransferase (SPT) complex, which uses serine and palmitoyl-coenzyme A (CoA) as substrates to produce an 18-carbon sphingoid base. The constitutive SPT complex is a multi-subunit complex most commonly consisting of two large subunits, SPTLC1 and SPTLC2, and one or more small subunits (SPTSSA/SPTSSB) and the ORMDL regulatory subunits. By far the most abundant sphingolipids incorporate the 18-carbon dihydrosphingosine (DHS), of which the fatty chain includes the 16 carbons from palmitate plus 2 carbons from serine. Substitution of SPTLC2 with the inducible isoform SPTLC3 causes production of alternative sphingoid bases.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3602> "SPOTS complex")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3602> "SPT complex")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3602> "Serine palmitoyltransferase complex subunits")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3602> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3603> (Serine palmitoyltransferase long chain base subunits)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3603> "Mammalian serine palmitoyltransferase (SPT) has three core subunits: SPTLC1, SPTLC2, and SPTLC3. Whereas SPTLC1 and SPTLC2 are ubiquitously expressed, SPTLC3 expression is restricted to certain tissues only. SPTLC1 is essential for activity, whereas SPTLC2 and SPTLC3 are partly redundant but differ in their enzymatic properties.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3603> "SPT core subunits")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3603> "SPTLC")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3603> "Serine palmitoyltransferase long chain base subunits")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3603> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3602>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3604> (Serine palmitoyltransferase small subunits)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3604> "Serine palmitoyltransferase (SPT) catalyzes the first committed step in sphingolipid biosynthesis. The SPT complex includes small stimulatory subunits encoded by the paralogs SPTSSA and SPTSSB that are required for maximal enzyme activity and also confer distinct acyl-CoA substrate specificities. These are functionally equivalent, but not homologous, to yeast TSC3. The 2 SPTSS isoforms share a conserved hydrophobic central domain predicted to reside in the membrane, and each interacts with the large core subunits.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3604> "SPTSS")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3604> "Stimulatory SPT subunits")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3604> "ssSPTs")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3604> "Serine palmitoyltransferase small subunits")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3604> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3602>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3607> (Elastases)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3607> "Elastases are proteases capable of degrading mature cross-linked elastin. Elastases are found in different classes of protease: the matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs), aspartic proteases, serine proteases and cysteine proteases. Elastin cleavage by certain enzymes requires pre-damage of elastin, occurring as a result of aging processes, as has for instance been described for the serine protease human leukocyte elastase (ELANE) or metalloendopeptidase neprilysin (MME).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3607> "Elastases")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3607> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/362> (WD repeat domain containing)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/362> "WDR")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/362> "WD repeat domain containing")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/362> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3626> (Spectrin repeat containing)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3626> "The spectrin superfamily of proteins function to connect cytoskeletal elements to each other, the cell membrane, and the nucleus. The signature domain is the spectrin repeat, a 106–122-amino-acid segment comprising three α-helices. α-actinin is considered to be the ancestral protein and functions to cross-link actin filaments. It then evolved to generate spectrin and dystrophin that function to link the actin cytoskeleton to the cell membrane, as well as the spectraplakins and plakins that link cytoskeletal elements to each other and to junctional complexes. A final class comprises the nesprins, which are able to bind to the nuclear membrane.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3626> "Spectrin superfamily")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3626> "Spectrin repeat containing")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3626> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3628> (ADCK family)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3628> "The AarF domain-containing kinase (ADCK) family of proteins is a group of putative mitochondrial kinases believed to play a pivotal role in regulating mitochondrial functions and cellular metabolism. Currently, five isoforms of ADCK proteins have been identified. Although the precise functions of each isoform remain largely undefined, the ADCK kinase family is involved in coenzyme Q (CoQ) biosynthesis, maintenance of mitochondrial homeostasis, and tumorigenesis.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3628> "ABC1 kinases")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3628> "ADCK")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3628> "aarF domain containing kinase family")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3628> "ADCK family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3628> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/SO_0005855>)

